.TH GFAN 1 "2026-08-21" "gigactl 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gfan \- control the fans on Gigabyte G5/G6 laptops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gfan
.RI [ status ]
.br
.B gfan watch
.br
.B gfan auto
.br
.B gfan max
.br
.B gfan
.I percent
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B gfan
.I cpu-percent gpu-percent
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gfan
reads and sets the two laptop fans through the Embedded Controller: fan 1 cools
the CPU, fan 2 the GPU. With no argument it prints temperatures, duty cycle and
RPM.
.PP
It works in one of two modes, chosen automatically each time it runs.
.TP
.B daemon
The
.BR gigactld (8)
service owns its D\-Bus name, which makes it the single writer of the Embedded
Controller.
.B gfan
becomes an unprivileged client of it: no
.BR sudo ,
no
.BR ec_probe ,
and no chance of the two interleaving controller commands. polkit lets the user
of the active local session do this without a password.
.TP
.B direct
No daemon is running. Direct writes require explicit
.B GIGACTL_ALLOW_DIRECT=1
and the controller lifetime lease; they are not the default recovery strategy.
.B gfan
drives the controller itself and re\-runs itself under
.BR sudo (8)
to do so. Concurrent invocations take
.I /run/lock/gigactl\-ec.lock
\(em the same lock the daemon takes \(em so their command sequences cannot
interleave. This mode needs the
.B ec_probe
utility from nbfc\-linux.
.PP
If neither can be established \(em no
.BR busctl (1)
on the system, or the bus does not answer \(em
.B gfan
refuses to do anything rather than risk writing the controller behind a running
daemon's back.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Print CPU and GPU temperature, each fan's duty cycle and RPM, and the current
mode. This is the default when no argument is given.
.TP
.B watch
Print the same status every two seconds until interrupted.
.TP
.B auto
Request firmware-auto independently for both fans. A transport failure can
prevent handback; command acknowledgement is not physical ownership evidence.
.TP
.B max
Run both fans at 100%.
.TP
.I percent
Run both fans at the given duty cycle, 0 to 100.
.TP
.I cpu-percent gpu-percent
Run fan 1 at the first duty cycle and fan 2 at the second.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-force
Legacy syntax only; it must not bypass thermal protection. Software control
uses a 30% floor at 70 \(deC and 100% emergency requests at CPU 95 \(deC or GPU
87 \(deC. These are software policy thresholds, not measured firmware limits.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B gfan
exits 0 on success and 1 on failure: an unsupported machine, a refused duty
cycle, a controller that did not accept the write, a lock held by another
writer, or a daemon whose presence could not be determined.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GIGACTL_ALLOW_DIRECT
Set to 1 only for explicitly reviewed standalone writes. Unknown bus ownership
and controller lease contention still refuse access. Raw EC access may race the
kernel even for reads; userspace locking does not remove that risk.
.PP
There is no environment override for the vendor/model/BIOS write guard: writes
require a GIGABYTE G5 GE on BIOS FB06 or a GIGABYTE G6 KF, the same allowlist
the privileged daemon enforces. Reads are not model-gated but still hold the
controller lease.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/gigactl/owner.lock
Lifetime controller lease, held before the mailbox lock. A running daemon
retains it; direct clients recheck D-Bus ownership after acquiring it.
.TP
.I /run/gfan.mode
The manual duty cycle last set in direct mode, reported by
.BR "gfan status" .
Absent when the firmware is in control. Cleared on reboot.
.TP
.I /run/lock/gigactl\-ec.lock
Serialises Embedded Controller access between
.BR gfan ,
.BR gkbd (1)
and
.BR gigactld (8).
.SH NOTES
Verified on the Gigabyte G6 KF and the G5 GE on BIOS FB06. Writes to any
other model \(em including other G5, G6 and G7 variants \(em are refused:
hardware similarity is not evidence. A GPU temperature of 0 means the GPU
sensor is unavailable or asleep; it is ignored by the thermal guard rather
than treated as a fatal failure, while CPU data is always required.
.PP
In direct mode every duty cycle is read back after writing. If the controller
does not converge on the requested value,
.B gfan
attempts firmware recovery and reports failures. The daemon reconciles targets
against readback with bounded ramp deadlines; pending targets are not reported
as verified. New safety behavior is fake-tested, not hardware-validated.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gkbd (1),
.BR gigactl-gui (1),
.BR gigactld (8)
.PP
The reverse\-engineered controller protocol is documented in
.I /usr/share/doc/gigactl
and at
.UR https://github.com/smairio/gigactl
.UE .
